# Translation dictionaries: two branches that each add strings would conflict on every merge (they all append before the
# closing line). With merge=union git keeps the lines of both sides. That is safe because every entry is one line ending
# with a comma – also the last one before `];` / `};` – so the closing line is shared context and never doubled; a text
# with line breaks is written with "\n", never across lines.
# What union cannot see: both sides adding the same key, or changing the same entry differently (both versions stay).
# tools/tests/unit/3860-… fails on a duplicate key, an entry that spans lines and a last entry without its comma;
# php -l / the dictionary tests catch anything else. After a union merge, run php tools/unit-tests.php 3860.
# merge=union applies to merges made with a local git (git merge, rebase, cherry-pick); GitHub's merge button and
# conflict editor ignore it, so merge branches that touch dictionaries locally.
system/jazyky/*.php merge=union
image/jazyky/*.js merge=union
